<p>Mali&#x27;s military leader Assimi Goita appeared in public Tuesday for the first time since coordinated weekend attacks by al-Qaida-linked JNIM and the Tuareg-led Azawad Liberation Front struck targets including Bamako, Kati, Gao, Sevare and Kidal. Reports citing Malian and foreign officials said the attacks killed Defence Minister Sadio Camara and targeted military sites, infrastructure and areas near Bamako&#x27;s airport. Goita&#x27;s office said he met Russian Ambassador Igor Gromyko in Bamako to discuss the security situation and Mali&#x27;s partnership with Russia. Russia&#x27;s Defence Ministry said its Africa Corps, which replaced Wagner in Mali, helped prevent what it called an attempted coup. It also said its fighters in Kidal fought for more than 24 hours while surrounded and outnumbered. The Guardian said Russia provided no evidence for its allegation that European and Ukrainian instructors trained the attackers. Kidal remained disputed after Tuareg fighters said they had taken the northern city following the withdrawal of Malian and Russian-backed forces. Mali&#x27;s Prime Minister Abdoulaye Maiga said the attackers sought to sow fear, break national cohesion and derail the transition process.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/m4Cq3KRWZytwvSa8YWAIsO5jMGxQ4SdmODtcrOy9doLdIDpwCWb9fr6d0uoodtbEVTFd0qHqJ3402UlfD_6oYNdPIdE9_EO855n9o_tSHjfxBuANZrqjBEXyQuHs_qk8oHT2Rkdg6z0ME1WAB4MfVk7ryWIaEgoWPY2kbzADeg' alt='Image accompanying AP&#x27;s explainer on the coordinated militant and rebel attacks in Mali.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Scale: ABC News described the weekend offensive as Mali&#x27;s largest coordinated attack in more than a decade, larger in scale than earlier raids.</li><li>Kidal withdrawal: Le Monde reported that at least 400 Africa Corps paramilitaries were evacuated from Kidal under armed escort.</li><li>Bamako pressure: Libération reported that a JNIM spokesperson threatened a blockade of the Malian capital after Goita&#x27;s reappearance.</li><li>Operations unclear: Eastleigh Voice said the Russian-backed unit reported continuing military operations elsewhere in Mali without specifying locations, aims or timing.</li></ul><blockquote>It is evident that only the support of sponsors enabled these cowardly and barbaric acts - Abdoulaye Maiga</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mali&#x27;s military government: Prime Minister Abdoulaye Maiga said Malian forces had repelled the coordinated offensive and urged the public not to lose resolve. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/africa/639209-terrorists-attempted-mali-military-government-overthrow/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Russia&#x27;s Defence Ministry: The ministry said Africa Corps avoided mass civilian casualties and inflicted &quot;irreplaceable losses&quot; on insurgents, while not specifying a casualty toll. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/28/russia-claims-its-africa-corps-group-prevented-coup-in-mali-after-rebels-seize-towns'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Azawad Liberation Front: The Tuareg-led movement seeks autonomy or independence for northern Mali&#x27;s Azawad, a different objective from JNIM&#x27;s Islamist project. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/28/what-is-the-azawad-liberation-front-part-of-mali-attacks'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Senior Malian official cited by RFI: A senior Malian official accused Russian forces of &quot;betrayal,&quot; highlighting tensions inside Bamako&#x27;s security partnership with Moscow. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604280333.html'>AllAfrica</a>)</li><li>Justyna Gudzowska, The Sentry: Gudzowska said the offensive showed JNIM&#x27;s reach and sent a message to Malians, regional capitals and foreign partners that the group can operate inside the state&#x27;s supposedly secure core. (<a href='https://www.sabcnews.com/sabcnews/mali-at-risk-of-splintering-after-jihadi-and-separatist-attacks/'>SABC News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/